%======================================================================
\chapter{System Overview}
%======================================================================

DIFS (Differentiated-QoS Inference Serving) is a multi-model Stable Diffusion inference serving system with gRPC-based scheduling, autoscaling (horizontal/vertical/hybrid), and workload classification. This guide focuses on the two-model configuration comparing SD 2.1 and SD 3.5 Medium on an NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPU (48\,GB VRAM) using sequential CPU offload.

\section{Scaling Modes}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Horizontal Scaling:} Independent workers are added or removed. Each worker runs its own process and serves requests on its own gRPC port.
    \item \textbf{Vertical Scaling:} Parameters such as batch size and worker concurrency are tuned on existing workers.
    \item \textbf{Hybrid Scaling:} Combines horizontal and vertical scaling simultaneously.
\end{itemize}

\section{Model Switching}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Hot Switching:} The model pointer in memory is swapped to a pre-loaded model ($\sim$0.1\,s). Implemented via \texttt{hot\_switch\_model()}.
    \item \textbf{Cold Switching:} The current model is unloaded and a new model is loaded from disk ($\sim$3--34\,s). Implemented via \texttt{cold\_switch\_model()}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Workload Classification}

Workloads are classified into three tiers based on prompt analysis:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Fast:} Simple prompts served by smaller models (SD 2.1).
    \item \textbf{Balanced:} Moderate prompts served by mid-tier models.
    \item \textbf{Quality:} Complex prompts requiring large models (SD 3.5 Medium).
\end{itemize}

\section{Scheduling Policy}

\textbf{Scheduling policy.} The scheduler is neither pure FIFO nor pure EDF; the policy is scaling-mode dependent. In \texttt{vertical} mode requests are served strictly first-come, first-served (FIFO) from the single \texttt{request\_queue}. In \texttt{horizontal} and \texttt{hybrid} modes requests are classified into three strict-priority classes (\textit{interactive} $>$ \textit{balanced} $>$ \textit{batch}) and, within each class, dispatched earliest-deadline-first (EDF) by SLO deadline ($\texttt{deadline} = \texttt{arrival} + \texttt{latency\_slo}/1000$). Worker selection is a separate least-loaded policy: \texttt{get\_next\_worker()} scores each candidate as $\texttt{active\_requests} \times 1000 + \texttt{avg\_latency\_ms} + \texttt{gpu\_memory\_mb} \times 0.05$, plus $1000$ if the worker is busy and $5000$ on workload-class mismatch, and selects the minimum. In DIFS\_OC\_v2 the policy is identical; the INFaaS extension adds per-model replica tracking (\texttt{get\_replica\_count()}) and a unified \texttt{scale\_infaas()} autoscaler pass, but does not alter the scheduler's queueing or dispatch policy.

\section{Scaling Decision Strategy}

DIFS\_OC\_v2 employs a dual-threshold decision strategy:

\subsection{$\rho$-Based Horizontal Scaling}
The autoscaler monitors $\rho = \frac{\text{arrival rate}}{\text{service rate} \times \text{worker count}}$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\rho > 0.80$: Scale up (add a new worker)
  \item $\rho < 0.30$: Scale down (remove idle worker after cooldown)
\end{itemize}
The hysteresis band ($0.30$--$0.80$) prevents oscillatory behaviour.

\subsection{INFaaS Cost-Model (Strategies A vs B)}
For vertical model-switching, the autoscaler compares two strategies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Strategy A}: Sum of per-variant costs --- $\sum_{m} (\text{load\_time}_m \times \text{count}_m + \text{load\_time}_m / \text{QPS}_m)$
  \item \textbf{Strategy B}: Fastest variant cost --- $\min_{m} \text{load\_time}_m \times \text{total\_workers}$
  \item \textbf{Decision}: If cost$_A > 1.5 \times$ cost$_B$, switch to the fastest variant; otherwise keep the current mix.
\end{itemize}
The $1.5\times$ threshold prevents unnecessary switching when the benefit is marginal.

