\chapter{How to Reproduce the Codebase}
%======================================================================

\section{Environment Setup}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Environment setup}]
conda create -n DIFS_OC_v2 python=3.10
conda activate DIFS_Gen_OC_v2
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install -r requirements-torch.txt

# Install protobuf compiler
pip install grpcio-tools
python -m grpc_tools.protoc -I protos \
    --python_out=. --grpc_python_out=. \
    protos/query.proto protos/infaas_request_status.proto
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Model Setup}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Model download and symlinks}]
# Download models (use ungated mirrors)
# SD 2.1: sd2-community/stable-diffusion-2-1
# SD 3.5 Medium: camenduru/stable-diffusion-3.5-medium

# Create symlinks
mkdir -p /tmp/model
ln -sf /path/to/stable-diffusion-2-1 /tmp/model/
ln -sf /path/to/stable-diffusion-3.5-medium /tmp/model/

# Copy FID reference stats
cp real_stats.npz /tmp/model/
\end{lstlisting}

%======================================================================
\chapter{Generating Plots}
%======================================================================

\section{Two-Model Plot Generation}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Generate two-model plots}]
cd DiffServ_0112_1730_coco2/0112_1730_coco
python src/plots/generate_two_model_plots.py

# Output location
ls results/model_benchmark/two_models_plots/
ls results/model_benchmark/two_models_plot_data/
\end{lstlisting}

This generates 16 plots (8 bar + 5 line + 3 switching) filtered to SD-2.1 and SD-3.5-Medium only, without overwriting the all-model plots.

\section{Plot Data Files}

Filtered CSVs reside in \texttt{results/model\_benchmark/two\_models\_plot\_data/}. Bar chart CSVs use long format. Line plot CSVs use wide format.

%======================================================================
\chapter{Running Benchmarks}
%======================================================================

\section{Two-Model Benchmark}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Two-model benchmark}]
python src/experiments/run_model_benchmark.py \
  --models 3 7 \
  --modes horizontal vertical hybrid \
  --workload 5 --steps 10
\end{lstlisting}

Model IDs: SD-2.1 = 3, SD-3.5-Medium = 7.

\section{Benchmark Output Structure}

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Output directory layout}]
results/model_benchmark/
  horizontal/model_<name>/metrics/*.csv
  vertical/model_<name>/metrics/*.csv
  hybrid/model_<name>/metrics/*.csv
  benchmark_summary.csv
  plots/*.png              # all-model plots
  two_models_plots/*.png   # two-model plots
  plot_data/*.csv           # all-model CSVs
  two_models_plot_data/*.csv # two-model CSVs
\end{lstlisting}

%======================================================================
\chapter{Major Files to Edit}
%======================================================================

\section{Adding a New Model}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Edit \texttt{src/model\_registry.py}: add entry to \texttt{ALL\_MODELS} dict.
    \item Edit \texttt{src/models.json}: add ID-to-filename mapping.
    \item Create symlink in \texttt{/tmp/model/}.
    \item Add to \texttt{experiment\_config.py} \texttt{MODELS} list.
    \item Re-run benchmark.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Changing Scaling Mode}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Edit \texttt{src/config.py}: \texttt{SCALING\_MODE}.
    \item Or pass \texttt{--modes} flag to benchmark runner.
\end{enumerate}

%======================================================================
\chapter{Potholes and Pitfalls}
%======================================================================

\section{Coding Pitfalls}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Proto stubs:} Regenerate after editing \texttt{query.proto}.
    \item \textbf{Import paths:} Always run from project root.
    \item \textbf{Port conflicts:} Port 50050 (scheduler) and 50060+ (workers) must be free.
    \item \textbf{Model cache:} \texttt{/tmp} is cleared on reboot. Re-create symlinks.
    \item \textbf{CUDA OOM:} Use \texttt{enable\_sequential\_cpu\_offload=True} for memory-intensive models.
\end{itemize}

\section{Inference Pitfalls}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Model loading time:} SD 3.5 Medium takes $\sim$7--34\,s, SD 2.1 takes $\sim$3--6\,s.
    \item \textbf{Sampler compatibility:} SD 3.5 uses FlowMatch Euler; SD 2.1 uses Euler Ancestral.
    \item \textbf{CPU offload overhead:} Required for 48GB VRAM with large models.
\end{itemize}

\section{Testing Pitfalls}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Benchmark isolation:} Start fresh for each run.
    \item \textbf{Autoscaler interference:} Disable during benchmarking (\texttt{DIFS\_NO\_AUTOSCALER=1}).
    \item \textbf{Process cleanup:}
\begin{lstlisting}
pkill -f pytorch_container_diffusion
pkill -f server.py
\end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

%======================================================================
% Chapter 9: SLO Extension -- What Changed vs. the Original Codebase
%======================================================================
\chapter{SLO Extension: What Changed vs.\ the Original Codebase}

This chapter documents every change made to the original DIFS\_OC\_v2 codebase to add the two-SLO (latency + accuracy) evaluation system. It is intended for (a) reviewers who need to see exactly which modules diverged from the original code, and (b) developers who want to re-implement the SLO pipeline from scratch. The changes mirror the DIFS\_OC\_v1 SLO extension module-for-module; the only behavioural difference is that the SLO feedback enters the autoscaler through the unified \texttt{scale\_infaas()} pass rather than through separate \texttt{scale()} paths.

\section{Changed vs.\ Original Modules}

The SLO extension modified the following existing modules. In every case the original behavior is preserved and the SLO logic is added alongside it; no original feature was removed.

\begin{longtable}{p{4.2cm} p{5.3cm} p{5.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Module} & \textbf{Original Behavior} & \textbf{SLO Change} \\
\midrule
\endfirsthead
\toprule
\textbf{Module} & \textbf{Original Behavior} & \textbf{SLO Change} \\
\midrule
\endhead
\texttt{protos/query.proto} & \texttt{QueryOnlineImageRequest} fields 1--6 (prompt, steps, sampler, etc.) & Field \texttt{string AccuracyLevel = 7;} added. Both \texttt{protos/query\_pb2.py} and \texttt{src/query\_pb2.py} regenerated with \texttt{grpc\_tools.protoc}. \\
\texttt{src/config.py} & No SLO settings; \texttt{SLA\_TARGETS} only & Added \texttt{SLO\_LATENCY\_MULTIPLIER = 2.5}, \texttt{ACCURACY\_STEP\_RANGES}, \texttt{ACCURACY\_STEP\_MAP}, \texttt{DEFAULT\_ACCURACY\_LEVEL = "medium"}, \texttt{SLO\_VIOLATION\_BUDGET = 0.01}. \\
\texttt{src/client\_async\_v3.py} & \texttt{-steps} only & Added \texttt{-accuracy \{high,medium,low\}} argument; maps level to step count and sets \texttt{request.accuracy\_level}. \\
\texttt{src/scheduler.py} & Queues keyed by (model, accuracy), round-robin pick, no deadline & Added \texttt{slo\_stats} dict, \texttt{\_slo\_context()}, \texttt{\_record\_slo()}, \texttt{get\_slo\_stats()}, \texttt{get\_slo\_violation\_rate()}; sets \texttt{request.deadline = arrival + slo\_ms/1000} at enqueue; SLO eval in \texttt{dispatch\_request()} success path and \texttt{handle\_request()} batch loop. \\
\texttt{src/worker\_pool.py} & \texttt{dequeue()} pops per queue in order & \texttt{dequeue()} now drains each priority queue and picks \texttt{min(candidates, key=\_deadline)} (EDF); \texttt{get\_batch()} sorts the batch by deadline. \\
\texttt{src/autoscaler.py} & Scale decisions from utilization $\rho$ only, via unified \texttt{scale\_infaas()} & Added \texttt{slo\_breached} trigger inside \texttt{scale\_infaas()}: when the rolling violation rate exceeds \texttt{SLO\_VIOLATION\_BUDGET}, it forces scale-up even if $\rho \le 0.80$. \\
\texttt{src/metrics.py} & \texttt{log\_latency}, \texttt{log\_gpu\_memory} etc. & Added \texttt{record\_slo()} appending rows to \texttt{metrics/slo\_metrics.csv}. \\
\texttt{src/experiments/experiment\_config.py} & \texttt{LOAD\_LEVELS} for the classic load study & Added \texttt{SLO\_LOAD\_LEVELS = \{"low": 2/60, "medium": 4/60, "high": 6/60\}} (2/4/6\,req/min), \texttt{SLO\_PROMPT\_COUNT = 300}, per-level \texttt{SLO\_WINDOW\_SEC\_BY\_LEVEL} (window $=300/\lambda$), \texttt{SLO\_CLIENT\_TIMEOUT\_MULT = 4.0}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\section{Modules Added}

The following modules are new; they do not exist in the original codebase.

\begin{longtable}{p{5.5cm} p{9.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{New File} & \textbf{Purpose} \\
\midrule
\endfirsthead
\toprule
\textbf{New File} & \textbf{Purpose} \\
\midrule
\endhead
\texttt{src/slo.py} & Pure-python SLO library: \texttt{ACCURACY\_STEP\_MAP}, \texttt{resolve\_latency\_slo\_ms(model, steps)}, \texttt{resolve\_step\_count()}, \texttt{get\_accuracy\_level\_from\_steps()}, \texttt{load\_exec\_profiling\_data()}. \\
\texttt{src/experiments/generate\_slo\_profiling.py} & Measures execution time per (model, steps) and writes \texttt{results/model\_benchmark/plot\_data\_5model/slo\_exec\_profiling.csv}. \\
\texttt{src/experiments/generate\_slo\_benchmark\_results.py} & Discrete-event simulation: Poisson arrivals of 300 COCO prompts per load level (window $=300/\lambda$: 150/75/17\,min), 5 models $\times$ 3 accuracy levels, single serial worker, 12 rounds averaged. Writes \texttt{slo\_benchmark\_results.csv}. \\
\texttt{src/experiments/run\_slo\_benchmark.py} & Optional live-GPU runner; merges measured \texttt{metrics/slo\_metrics.csv} rows with the simulated rows. \\
\texttt{src/plots/generate\_slo\_plots.py} & Produces \texttt{slo\_latency\_attainment.png}, \texttt{slo\_accuracy\_attainment.png}, \texttt{slo\_throughput.png}. \\
\texttt{results/model\_benchmark/plot\_data\_5model/slo\_exec\_profiling.csv} & Profiling data: measured 20-step exec times, linearly extrapolated 30/40-step times. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

\section{How to Implement the SLO System From Scratch}

If you are rebuilding the system from an unmodified DIFS codebase, follow these steps in order.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=2pt]
    \item \textbf{Add the proto field.} Append \texttt{string AccuracyLevel = 7;} to \texttt{QueryOnlineImageRequest} in \texttt{protos/query.proto}, then regenerate:
\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Regenerate pb2 files}]
python -m grpc_tools.protoc -I protos \
  --python_out=protos --grpc_python_out=protos protos/query.proto
python -m grpc_tools.protoc -I protos \
  --python_out=src --grpc_python_out=src protos/query.proto
\end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{Add the SLO config block} to \texttt{src/config.py}: the 2.5$\times$ latency multiplier, the accuracy step ranges/map, the default level, and the violation budget (0.01).
    \item \textbf{Write \texttt{src/slo.py}.} Implement \texttt{resolve\_latency\_slo\_ms()} as $2.5 \times \texttt{exec\_time(model, steps)}$ reading \texttt{slo\_exec\_profiling.csv}, \texttt{resolve\_step\_count()} to map a level to a step count, and \texttt{get\_accuracy\_level\_from\_steps()} to reverse-map a served step count back to a level (canonical 20/30/40 first, then range membership).
    \item \textbf{Extend the client.} Add \texttt{-accuracy}, resolve it to steps via \texttt{slo.resolve\_step\_count()}, and set \texttt{accuracy\_level} on the request.
    \item \textbf{Hook the scheduler.} In \texttt{enqueue()}, compute the latency SLO and set \texttt{request.deadline = arrival\_time + slo\_ms/1000}. In \texttt{dequeue()}, pick the earliest deadline across the per-class queues (EDF); in \texttt{get\_batch()}, sort by deadline. Add \texttt{\_slo\_context()} to wrap each dispatch, \texttt{\_record\_slo()} to compare end-to-end latency against the deadline, and \texttt{get\_slo\_violation\_rate()} for the rolling window.
    \item \textbf{Record metrics.} Add \texttt{metrics.record\_slo()} and call it from the scheduler's \texttt{\_record\_slo()}.
    \item \textbf{Add the autoscaler trigger.} Inside \texttt{scale\_infaas()}, check \texttt{get\_slo\_violation\_rate() > SLO\_VIOLATION\_BUDGET} and force scale-up when breached, in addition to the $\rho > 0.80$ rule.
    \item \textbf{Profile execution times.} Run \texttt{generate\_slo\_profiling.py} to produce the per-(model, steps) exec-time CSV.
    \item \textbf{Build the benchmark + plots.} Run \texttt{generate\_slo\_benchmark\_results.py} for the simulated results and \texttt{generate\_slo\_plots.py} for the three SLO graphs.
\end{enumerate}

